\section{Result columns}{


Columns of a result set are mapped to the Arrow types that keep the
values of the database types intact:
\itemize{
\item \code{BIT} to \code{bool}.
\item \code{TINYINT}, \code{SMALLINT}, and \code{INTEGER} to \code{int32}.
\item \code{BIGINT} to \code{int64}. The \code{bigint} argument of \code{\link[DBI:dbConnect]{DBI::dbConnect()}} only
applies to data frames.
\item \code{REAL}, \code{FLOAT}, and \code{DOUBLE} to \code{double}.
\item \code{DECIMAL} and \code{NUMERIC} to \code{decimal128}, or \code{decimal256} for more than
38 digits, with the precision and scale reported by the driver, keeping
all digits. Values with more fractional digits than the reported scale
are truncated, with a warning. Columns for which the driver doesn't
report a usable precision are mapped to \code{double}.
\item \code{DATE} to \code{date32}.
\item \code{TIME} to \code{time64} with microsecond precision, or nanosecond precision
when the driver reports more than six fractional digits (SQL Server
\code{time(7)}), keeping fractional seconds.
\item \code{TIMESTAMP}, and SQL Server \code{DATETIMEOFFSET}, to \code{timestamp} with
microsecond precision. As for data frames, values are interpreted in the
\code{timezone} of the connection, unless the type itself carries an offset,
and are labelled with the \code{timezone_out} of the connection.
\item Character types to \code{utf8}, converted from the \code{encoding} of the connection
if needed.
\item Binary types to \code{binary}.
\item Other types to \code{utf8}.
}
}

\section{Parameters}{


Arrow arrays are bound to query parameters as follows:
\itemize{
\item \code{bool} and integers with up to 32 bits as 32-bit integers, \code{int64} and
\code{uint32} as 64-bit integers, \code{uint64} as unsigned 64-bit integers.
\item Floating point types as \code{double}.
\item Decimal types as strings holding their exact decimal representation.
\item \code{utf8}, \code{large_utf8}, \code{utf8_view}, and dictionaries of these types as
strings, converted to the \code{encoding} of the connection if needed.
\item \code{binary}, \code{large_binary}, \code{fixed_size_binary}, and \code{binary_view} as binary
data.
\item \code{date32} and \code{date64} as \code{DATE}.
\item \code{time32}, \code{time64}, and \code{duration} as \code{TIME}, keeping fractional seconds.
\item \code{timestamp} with a time zone as \code{TIMESTAMP}, expressed in the \code{timezone}
of the connection, or with its own time zone when the target is a SQL
Server \code{DATETIMEOFFSET}. \code{timestamp} without a time zone is written as is.
\item \code{null} as \code{NULL}.
}

Nested types are not supported.
}

\section{Chunking}{


\code{dbFetchArrow()} fetches all remaining rows before returning, as an array
stream made of arrays of at most \code{chunk_size} rows each; an array is also
cut short once a string or binary column holds 1 GiB of data.
\code{dbFetchArrowChunk()} returns one such array per call, and an empty array
once all rows have been fetched.

When binding, the statement is executed once for each array of the stream,
in batches of at most \code{batch_rows} rows. For queries, only the rows returned
by the last execution can be fetched afterwards.
}

\examples{
\dontrun{
library(DBI)
con <- dbConnect(odbc::odbc(), dsn = "PostgreSQL")
dbWriteTableArrow(con, "mtcars", mtcars, temporary = TRUE)

# A nanoarrow array stream, convert with as.data.frame() or
# arrow::as_arrow_table()
stream <- dbReadTableArrow(con, "mtcars")
as.data.frame(stream)

# Fetch in chunks
rs <- dbSendQueryArrow(con, "SELECT * FROM mtcars")
while (!dbHasCompleted(rs)) {
  chunk <- dbFetchArrowChunk(rs, chunk_size = 10)
  print(nrow(as.data.frame(chunk)))
}
dbClearResult(rs)

# Bind parameters from an Arrow stream
rs <- dbSendQueryArrow(con, "SELECT * FROM mtcars WHERE cyl = ?")
dbBindArrow(rs, data.frame(cyl = 4))
as.data.frame(dbFetchArrow(rs))
dbClearResult(rs)

dbDisconnect(con)
}
}
